---
/**
 * Portfolio three: six funds that lean the stock holdings toward cheap, small and
 * profitable companies. The verdict is "probably", and the page carries both the gap
 * and the losing stretch inside it.
 */
import AllocationBar from "~/components/AllocationBar.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import HoldingsTable from "~/components/HoldingsTable.astro";
import KeyNumbers from "~/components/KeyNumbers.astro";
import { portfolio } from "~/content/portfolios";
import { figureValue } from "~/lib/figures";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const p = portfolio("value-lean");

const NUMBERS = [
  {
    value: await figureValue("value-lean-gap"),
    label: "a year over a cheap index mix",
    note: "1990 to 2026. Range 0.4 to 1.3.",
  },
  {
    value: await figureValue("value-lean-gap-out-of-sample"),
    label: "a year on data the choice was not made from",
    note: "1981 to 1990. Range 0.4 to 1.3.",
  },
  { value: "30 years", label: "of results to be confident it is not luck" },
  {
    value: await figureValue("value-drawdown"),
    label: "Cheap US stocks against the whole market",
    note: "Since September 2008, 17.7 years, still not recovered",
  },
];

const HOLDINGS = [
  { ticker: "VTI", what: "The whole US market", weight: "49%", fee: "0.03%" },
  { ticker: "VXUS", what: "Every market outside the US", weight: "16%", fee: "0.05%" },
  { ticker: "VTV", what: "Large US companies that are cheap relative to their earnings and assets", weight: "15%", fee: "0.03%" },
  { ticker: "AVDV", what: "Small, cheap, profitable companies in developed markets outside the US", weight: "10%", fee: "0.36%" },
  { ticker: "IDMO", what: "Developed-market companies outside the US whose shares have been rising", weight: "5%", fee: "0.25%" },
  { ticker: "AVES", what: "Cheap companies in emerging markets", weight: "5%", fee: "0.36%" },
];
---

<Base
  title="A lean toward value"
  description="Six funds that lean toward cheaper, smaller and more profitable companies. What the lean has added, how long it can lose for, and which fund to drop first."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Portfolio 2 of 3"
    title="A lean toward value"
    lede="The market, with a thumb on the scale toward companies that are cheap, small and profitable. A bet with a long record and a long losing stretch inside it."
  >
    <KeyNumbers items={NUMBERS} />
  </Hero>

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2>What you hold</h2>
  </div>

  <HoldingsTable rows={HOLDINGS} class="my-4" />
  <AllocationBar holdings={p.holdings} name={p.name} class="my-6" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The fee works out to 0.09% a year, or $9 on $10,000. Roughly two thirds of the money is still the plain
    market. The other third is the lean.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Why cheap, small and profitable</h2>

    <p>Companies that are cheap relative to what they earn have, over long stretches and in most countries,
    returned more than expensive ones. Since 1994, after the idea was published and the easy money was gone,
    cheap stocks earned about <Figure id="value-premium-pooled" inline showUnit={false} /> a year more than
    expensive ones across the US, other rich countries and emerging markets together. Almost all of that came
    from outside the US. In the US alone the gap was 1.6 points, too small to tell from luck on its own.</p>

    <p>That is why most of the lean here is international. AVDV is the one fund that buys small, cheap, profitable
    companies in developed markets, the corner where the record is strongest. Adding it at 10% was worth about
    <Figure id="avdv-added" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, with a range of 0.05 to 0.56, and it was positive
    in every stretch I split the data into. AVES does the same in emerging markets, where cheapness has paid the most, although the fund is
    only five years old.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What the lean has added</h2>

    <p>Over 1990 to 2026 these six funds would have earned about <Figure id="value-lean-gap" inline showUnit={false} />
    a year over a cheap index mix holding the same split of US and international stocks. The plausible range
    is 0.4 to 1.3. The gap was positive in each of the five periods I cut the data into. On a separate set of
    data covering 1981 to 1990 that was not used to choose the funds, the same lean earned
    <Figure id="value-lean-gap-out-of-sample" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, with a range of 0.4 to 1.3.</p>

    <p>Two cautions. Funds deliver less of an idea than the paper series does: funds chasing cheap stocks earned
    2.84% a year against 7.76% for the academic index of cheap stocks, in one study of live funds. If the funds here fall short the same way,
    the 0.8 becomes about 0.6, or 0.3 in the harshest reading. And a gap this small against a portfolio this
    similar takes about thirty years of results to separate from luck. Nobody holding this will know it worked
    for a long time.</p>

    <p>Over thirty years, 0.8 points a year is worth about as much as saving an extra 2.6% of your starting balance
    every year.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What losing looks like</h2>

    <p>Cheap US stocks have run <Figure id="value-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> the whole US market
    since September 2008. That is 17.7 years, and they have not caught up. International stocks ran
    <Figure id="intl-drawdown" inline showUnit={false} /> US stocks over 18.2 years. Anyone holding this portfolio
    through those stretches watched a plain US index fund pull ahead, year after year, with no way to know
    whether the lean was broken or just out of favour.</p>

    <p>Do not hold this because you think you could sit through that. Hold it only if you have already sat through
    something like it and know how you behaved.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The fund to drop first</h2>

    <p>IDMO is the weakest holding. It buys shares that have been rising, which is a different idea from cheapness,
    and that is why it is here: one more idea in the mix. But funds chasing rising stocks have delivered the least
    of any idea on paper, 1.86% a year against 9.48%, and IDMO turns over its whole portfolio about once a year to
    do it. If you want a simpler portfolio, drop IDMO and put its 5% into VXUS. If you want a simpler one still,
    drop AVES the same way. VTV, AVDV and VXUS are the ones to keep.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Should you stop here</h2>

    <p>Yes, if you want a lean with a long record and no borrowed money. The
    <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">next portfolio</a> keeps all six of these funds and adds one that does
    something none of them do. That fund adds an idea on top of stocks rather than swapping stocks for it, and it
    is also the youngest and least certain holding here.</p>

    <p>Figures on this page are before tax and after fund costs. What the lean is worth to you depends on your
    accounts, since IDMO, AVES and AVDV cost the most in tax outside a retirement account.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
